Certainly! Below is a simple Java program that implements a basic calculator capable of handling fractions and performing addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. This example uses the `BigDecimal` class for precise arithmetic and the `BigInteger` class for handling the numerator and denominator of fractions.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class FractionalCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Welcome to the Fractional Calculator!");
        System.out.print("Enter the first fraction (a/b): ");
        String firstFraction = scanner.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter the second fraction (c/d): ");
        String secondFraction = scanner.nextLine();

        BigDecimal firstFractionBigDecimal = parseFraction(firstFraction);
        BigDecimal secondFractionBigDecimal = parseFraction(secondFraction);

        System.out.print("Enter an operation (+, -, *, /): ");
        String operation = scanner.nextLine();

        BigDecimal result = null;

        switch (operation) {
            case "+":
                result = firstFractionBigDecimal.add(secondFractionBigDecimal);
                break;
            case "-":
                result = firstFractionBigDecimal.subtract(secondFractionBigDecimal);
                break;
            case "*":
                result = firstFractionBigDecimal.multiply(secondFractionBigDecimal);
                break;
            case "/":
                result = firstFractionBigDecimal.divide(secondFractionBigDecimal, 10, BigDecimal.ROUND_HALF_UP);
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid operation. Please use +, -, *, or /.");
                return;
        }

        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }

    private static BigDecimal parseFraction(String fraction) {
        String[] parts = fraction.split("/");
        BigInteger numerator = new BigInteger(parts[0].trim());
        BigInteger denominator = new BigInteger(parts[1].trim());
        return new BigDecimal(numerator).divide(new BigDecimal(denominator), BigDecimal.ROUND_HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

This program works as follows:
1. It prompts the user to input two fractions in the form of `a/b` and `c/d`.
2. It then asks for the desired operation (+, -, *, /).
3. Based on the operation, it performs the corresponding arithmetic operation on the fractions and displays the result.

You can compile and run this program using any Java development environment. Note that this example assumes the fractions are valid and in the correct format. You might want to add error handling for invalid inputs in a production-level application.

