model_response:
O erro "incompatible types" ocorre porque você está tentando usar um `GeckoResult<PromptResponse>` como se fosse um `PromptResponse`. No seu código, você está lidando com uma `CompletableFuture` que, quando concluída, retornará um `GeckoResult<PromptResponse>`. No entanto, você está tentando completar o `GeckoResult` com um valor `PromptResponse`, o que não é permitido.

Para corrigir o erro, você precisa esperar o resultado dentro do `CompletableFuture` e, em seguida, completar o `GeckoResult` com esse resultado. Aqui está o código corrigido:

```java
@Override
public GeckoResult<PromptResponse> onFilePrompt(GeckoSession session, FilePrompt prompt) {
    // Seu código existente para iniciar a ação de escolha de arquivo
    Intent intent = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_GET_CONTENT);
    intent.setType("*/*");
    intent.addCategory(Intent.CATEGORY_OPENABLE);
    currentFilePrompt = prompt;
    filePromptFuture = new CompletableFuture<>();

    // Suponha que "this" seja um Actividade ou Fragment
    startActivityForResult(intent, FILE_UPLOAD_REQUEST_CODE);

    // Crie um novo GeckoResult
    GeckoResult<PromptResponse> geckoResult = new GeckoResult<>();

    // Quando o CompletableFuture for concluído, complete o GeckoResult
    filePromptFuture.whenComplete((result, throwable) -> {
        if (throwable != null) {
            // Se houver um throwable, complete o GeckoResult excepcionalmente
            geckoResult.completeExceptionally(throwable);
        } else {
            // Em caso de sucesso, obtenha o PromptResponse do result e complete o GeckoResult
            PromptResponse promptResponse = result.get(); // Obtenha o PromptResponse do result
            geckoResult.complete(promptResponse);
        }
    });

    return geckoResult;
}
```

O principal ajuste que fizé aqui foi substituir `geckoResult.future.get()` por `result.get()`, já que `result` é agora o `CompletableFuture<PromptResponse>` que será retornado pelo método `whenComplete`. Assim, você obtém o valor `PromptResponse` correto e o completa no `GeckoResult`.

If you want to force a text field to display multiple columns, you generally have a few options in Adobe Flash/Animate with ActionScript 3. However, the Flash platform is now considered obsolete, and Adobe officially ended support for Flash Player on December 31, 2020. If you are working on a legacy project, here is a basic example of how you might use ActionScript to achieve a multi-column text field:

1. Create a new TextField in the Flash/Animate IDE.
2. Set the text field to multiline by setting the `multiline` property to true.
3. Adjust the `wordWrap` property to ensure that text wraps correctly.

Here's an example of ActionScript 3 code to achieve this:

```actionscript
import flash.text.TextField;
import flash.text.TextFormat;

// Create a new TextField
var textField:TextField = new TextField();

// Set the properties
textField.width = 200; // Set the width of the TextField
textField.multiline = true; // Allow multiple lines
textField.wordWrap = true; // Enable word wrapping

// Create a TextFormat object to style the text
var textFormat:TextFormat = new TextFormat();
textFormat.font = "Arial";
textFormat.size = 12;
textField.defaultTextFormat = textFormat;

// Add the TextField to the stage
addChild(textField);

// Add some sample text
textField.text = "This is some sample text that should wrap across multiple columns. " +
                 "You can add more text here to see how it wraps and displays multiple lines.";

// Center the TextField on the stage
textField.x = (stage.stageWidth - textField.width) / 2;
textField.y = (stage.stageHeight - textField.height) / 2;
```

However, if you are looking to create a grid-like text field, you might need to create multiple TextFields manually and position them according to the number of columns you want. Here is a basic example to create a 2-column grid-like text field:

```actionscript
import flash.text.TextField;
import flash.text.TextFormat;

// Create a container for the columns
var columns:Array = [];
var columnWidth:Number = 200;
var columnSpacing:Number = 10;

// Create two columns
for (var i:int = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    var column:TextField = new TextField();
    column.width = columnWidth;
    column.x = i * (columnWidth + columnSpacing);
    column.y = 0;
    column.multiline = true;
    column.wordWrap = true;
    column.border = true; // Just for visibility
    columns.push(column);
}

// Add columns to the stage
for each (var column:TextField in columns) {
    addChild(column);
}

// Add sample text to the columns
columns[0].text = "This is some sample text for column 1. " +
                  "You can add more text here to see how it wraps and displays multiple lines.";
columns[1].text = "This is some sample text for column 2. " +
                  "You can add more text here to see how it wraps and displays multiple lines.";
```

This will create two columns on the stage, each with its own text.

Since Adobe Flash is no longer supported, you might want to consider using HTML5, CSS, and JavaScript for modern web applications. Text layout and styling in web development are typically handled by CSS, and JavaScript can be used to dynamically manipulate the DOM. For instance, you can use CSS Grid or Flexbox to create multi-column layouts and JavaScript to dynamically update the content.

Sure, here is a simple Python code to determine if a given number is a prime number or not:

```python
def is_prime(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return False
    if n <= 3:
        return True
    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:
        return False
    i = 5
    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:
            return False
        i += 6
    return True

# Example usage:
number = int(input("Enter a number: "))
if is_prime(number):
    print(f"{number} is a prime number.")
else:
    print(f"{number} is not a prime number.")
```

This function `is_prime(n)` checks whether a number `n` is prime. It first handles simple cases (numbers less than or equal to 3) and then uses a loop to check divisibility by all numbers up to the square root of `n`. If `n` is divisible by any of these numbers, it is not prime. If no divisors are found, then `n` is prime.

