In C, error handling is typically done by returning error codes from functions. The convention is to define an enumeration type to represent the possible error states and then return a pointer to this enumeration type from the function. Here's an example of how to implement this:

1. Define the enumeration type for error codes:

```c
typedef enum {
    SUCCESS = 0,
    ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND,
    ERROR_INVALID_INPUT,
    ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY,
    ERROR_UNKNOWN,
    ERROR_COUNT // Keep this last to accommodate adding new errors
} ErrorCode;
```

2. Modify the function signature to return a pointer to the enumeration type:

```c
ErrorCode my_function(int input) {
    if (input < 0) {
        return ERROR_INVALID_INPUT;
    }

    // Other function logic here

    return SUCCESS;
}
```

3. In the calling code, check the returned value to handle errors:

```c
int main() {
    int input = 0;
    ErrorCode result = my_function(input);

    if (result != SUCCESS) {
        switch (result) {
            case ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND:
                printf("Error: File not found.\n");
                break;
            case ERROR_INVALID_INPUT:
                printf("Error: Invalid input.\n");
                break;
            case ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY:
                printf("Error: Out of memory.\n");
                break;
            case ERROR_UNKNOWN:
                printf("Error: Unknown error.\n");
                break;
            default:
                printf("An unexpected error occurred.\n");
                break;
        }
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

By using this approach, you can easily manage different error conditions and make your code more readable and maintainable.

